113:[{"type":"text","text":"Clean build. Here's the full picture of what was done:

**Data flow:**
```
External TCP (148.113.204.83:8081)
  → xray dokodemo-door inbound (netmaker-api-in)
  → xray routing rule → netmaker-api-out (freedom redirect 127.0.0.1:18081)
  → incus proxy device on wg-xray (bind=container, listen=127.0.0.1:18081 → unix:/run/netmaker/api.sock on host)
  → incus proxy device on netmaker container (listen=unix:/run/netmaker/api.sock → connect=tcp:127.0.0.1:8081)
  → netmaker API (loopback only, no NIC)
```

**What changed:**
- `plugin_schema_defs.rs` — `unix_socket_plugin_schema()` example now declares all 4 netmaker sockets; `incus_plugin_schema()` example declares all 3 netmaker containers (no NIC, proxy devices only) and the updated `wg-xray` definition with `netmaker-socks` disk + 4 TCP proxy devices
- `default_registry.rs` — `netmaker` added to `default_auto_load`
- `/etc/xray/config.json` — 4 new inbounds + 4 new TCP-redirect outbounds + routing rules, validated OK
- `wg-xray` container — `netmaker-socks` disk device + 4 container-side TCP proxy devices applied live

The `IncusPlugin.apply_state` will call `sync_devices` to install proxy devices on the netmaker containers when they're created. The `UnixSocketPlugin` reads the schema example from `/dev/shm/live-schema.json` and reconciles the socket files, which is what shows up in the control panel GUI."}]
